'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>When buying a whole Iberian ham, one of the most practical questions is <strong>which weight to choose</strong>. Shops commonly offer 6–7 kg, 7–8 kg and 8–9 kg bands. Bigger may look better value, but it is not automatically the best choice.</p>
<p>The right weight depends on household size, how often you eat ham, how quickly you will work through an opened piece and the usable yield you expect. Sometimes a shoulder or sliced format makes more sense than a larger whole ham.</p>
<h2>Piece weight is not sliced yield</h2>
<p>A whole ham includes bone, rind and external fat. An 8 kg ham therefore does not produce 8 kg of slices. Yield varies with anatomy, curing, fat level and cutting skill.</p>
<p>See our guide to <a href="/en/how-many-packs-from-an-iberian-ham-real-yield-of-a-whole-piece/">how many packs can be obtained from an Iberian ham</a> for a fuller explanation.</p>
<h2>What weights are common?</h2>
<p>Spanish Iberian-ham rules establish minimum finished weights: 100% Iberian hams must reach at least 5.75 kg, while other Iberian hams have a 7 kg minimum. Commercial pieces around 7–9 kg are therefore common.</p>
<p>Shoulder hams are smaller and can be easier for small households to finish, although their proportional meat yield is lower.</p>
<h2>One or two people</h2>
<p>If you eat ham only occasionally, vacuum-packed slices are usually the easiest format. If ham is part of daily breakfasts, dinners or aperitifs, a shoulder or smaller whole ham can make good sense.</p>
<h2>Three or four people</h2>
<p>For a family of three or four with regular consumption, a ham around <strong>7–8 kg</strong> is often a versatile choice: generous without forcing you to rush through a very large piece.</p>
<h2>Large households or frequent consumption</h2>
<p>For five or more people, or a household where ham is eaten almost every day, <strong>8–9 kg</strong> can be practical. Larger pieces are also common around Christmas and for frequent entertaining.</p>
<h2>Is a heavier ham always better?</h2>
<p>No. Weight alone <strong>does not define quality</strong>. Production category, Iberian breed percentage, raw material, curing and producer skill matter much more.</p>
<p>A superb 7 kg ham may be a better purchase than a larger piece from a lower category. See our guide to <a href="/en/acorn-fed-field-fattened-and-fattened-iberian-ham-differences/">acorn-fed, field-fattened and fattened Iberian ham</a>.</p>
<h2>Weight and curing</h2>
<p>Heavier pieces generally need more time for salt and moisture to evolve evenly. That does not mean you should always buy the biggest ham. Our guide to <a href="/en/how-long-is-iberian-ham-cured-and-what-determines-the-time/">how long Iberian ham is cured</a> explains the relationship.</p>
<h2>Ham or shoulder for a small household?</h2>
<p>A shoulder weighs much less and can be finished sooner, which is useful when one or two people still want to carve at home. The trade-off is a lower proportional meat yield.</p>
<h2>What happens if you buy too much?</h2>
<p>An opened ham does not suddenly expire, but the exposed cut gradually oxidises and dries. If progress through the piece is extremely slow, the final sections may be enjoyed below their best condition.</p>
<h2>Quick guide</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>1–2 people, occasional:</strong> sliced packs or shoulder.</li>
<li><strong>1–2 people, frequent:</strong> shoulder or smaller ham.</li>
<li><strong>3–4 people:</strong> roughly 7–8 kg.</li>
<li><strong>5+ people or very frequent consumption:</strong> 8–9 kg can be practical.</li>
<li><strong>Events:</strong> calculate portions first and combine a whole piece with sliced packs when useful.</li>
</ul>
<p>These are guidelines, not fixed rules. Two enthusiastic ham eaters can consume more than a family of five that serves it once a week.</p>
<h2>The right question</h2>
<p>Instead of asking which weight is “best”, ask <strong>which format and quantity your household can enjoy while the opened piece is still at its best</strong>. Then choose production category, producer and budget. Weight should fit the purchase to your home, not act as a quality score.</p>
